{"hash":"8c716a55a4254dda","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":92.815,"said":"Here it is: Bhim Shila. See its face, blackened by lamp smoke, daubed with vermilion, coins pressed into its cracks. People pray to this boulder. To understand why, go back to June 2013. For days, heavy rain had beaten on these mountains. On the evening of 16 June a landslide struck near the temple. Then, early next morning, Chorabari Tal, the glacier lake about six kilometres above us where the Mandakini is born, broke loose. Water, mud and boulders tore down this valley. Much of the town around the temple was swept away, and thousands of lives were lost across the region. Some pilgrims sheltered inside the temple through those hours, until rescuers could reach them. And this rock, carried down by the flood, came to rest right here, behind the shrine. The torrent split around it and passed on either side, and the temple stood. Eighty-six days later, on 11 September, the Rawal, Kedarnath’s chief priest, unlocked the doors, and worship began again with rites of purification. People named the boulder after Bhima, the strongest of five brothers you will soon meet. First, walk on behind the temple, through the gateway in the wall.\n\nDid you know? Chorabari Tal, the glacier lake that broke loose in 2013, is also called Gandhi Sarovar.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1144},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1160,"end":1248}],"words":[[0.098,0.397,0,4],[0.507,0.12,5,7],[0.639,0.3,8,10],[1.132,0.325,12,16],[1.468,0.505,17,22],[2.873,0.276,24,27],[3.161,0.228,28,31],[3.401,0.433,32,36],[4.099,0.457,38,47],[4.567,0.18,48,50],[4.76,0.337,51,55],[5.108,0.469,56,61],[5.817,0.361,63,69],[6.19,0.168,70,74],[6.37,0.601,75,84],[7.224,0.325,86,91],[7.56,0.373,92,99],[7.945,0.288,100,104],[8.245,0.18,105,108],[8.438,0.541,109,115],[9.88,0.312,117,123],[10.204,0.264,124,128],[10.481,0.144,129,131],[10.637,0.192,132,136],[10.841,0.529,137,144],[12.296,0.132,146,148],[12.44,0.565,149,